P['comex-force-majeure']=page('comex-force-majeure','COMEX Force Majeure Explained in Plain English',
 'What force majeure means in a COMEX gold or silver delivery context, what it is and is not, and which public data to watch. Plain English, no predictions.',
 'COMEX force majeure, explained', f'''<section><p><strong>Force majeure</strong> is French for "superior force." It's a clause in many contracts that lets a party delay or skip its obligations when extraordinary events outside its control make performance impossible, such as war, a natural disaster, a pandemic lockdown or a government order. It doesn't cancel the contract by default, and it isn't a penalty-free way out of a bad deal.</p></section>
<section><h2>What it would mean at COMEX</h2><p>COMEX is the CME Group exchange where gold and silver futures trade. Most contracts are closed before expiry, but a holder of an expiring contract can take delivery of metal from a COMEX-approved vault. The exchange's rulebook includes emergency and force majeure provisions. In a genuine emergency, these could allow delays or other changes to physical delivery instead of counting as a default. How any case is handled depends on the exchange's rules and decisions at the time.</p><p>In plain terms, force majeure is about <em>performance being impossible</em> because of an outside event, for example vaults being inaccessible or transport being shut down. A tight market or high prices are not the same thing.</p></section>
<section><h2>What it is not</h2><ul><li>It isn't a prediction. Explaining the clause doesn't mean we expect it to be used.</li><li>It isn't the same as a margin increase, position limits or a trading halt, which are other tools exchanges can use.</li><li>It isn't the same as cash settlement, which some other contracts use by design.</li></ul></section>
<section><h2>Public data people watch</h2><p>People who follow the physical side of the market look at a few public numbers. VaultPulse tracks each of them with dates and sources:</p><ul><li><strong>Registered inventory</strong>, the metal that can be delivered right now. COMEX silver registered was {F['s_reg']} as of {rd}. <a href="/comex-silver-inventory">Silver inventory →</a></li><li><strong>Deliveries</strong>, how much metal contract holders actually take. October silver deliveries so far were about {F['s_mtd']} ({F['s_mtd_d']}). <a href="/flow">Deliveries tracker →</a></li><li><strong>Retail premiums</strong>, what coins and bars cost above spot. <a href="/silver-premiums">Premiums →</a></li><li><strong>News</strong>, any official exchange notices. <a href="/gold-silver-news">News stream →</a></li></ul><p>No one of these numbers signals force majeure on its own. Official CME Group notices are the only reliable source for whether one has actually been declared.</p></section>
<section><h2>Where to check official notices</h2><p>CME Group posts exchange notices and its rulebook on its website. If you read a claim online that COMEX has declared force majeure, look for the notice on cmegroup.com before believing it.</p></section>
{links('/comex-silver-inventory','/comex-gold-inventory','/flow','/gold-silver-news')}
{SRC(('CME Group: Rulebook','https://www.cmegroup.com/rulebook/'),('CME Group: Notices','https://www.cmegroup.com/notices.html'),('Investopedia: Force majeure','https://www.investopedia.com/terms/f/forcemajeure.asp'))}''',
 [],
 faq=[('What does force majeure mean?','A contract clause that excuses or delays performance when extraordinary events beyond a party\'s control, such as war or natural disasters, make it impossible.'),
      ('Has COMEX declared force majeure on silver?','Check official CME Group notices for the current status. VaultPulse does not report rumours, and explaining the clause is not a prediction it will be used.')],
 crumb='COMEX force majeure')
P['paper-to-metal']=page('paper-to-metal','Paper to Metal: M2 &amp; M1 Gold/Silver Backing Calculator',
 f"How many dollars per ounce of US gold reserves or COMEX silver? Live M2/M1 backing calculator (M2 {F['m2t']}, {F['m2m']}). Hypothetical, sourced.",
 'Paper to metal: M2 and M1 backing calculators', f'''<section><p>This page asks a simple "what if" question: if every dollar in the US money supply had to be backed by metal, what price per ounce would that imply? Using M2 of {F['m2t']} ({F['m2m']}, FRED) and the US gold reserve of {F['goz']} troy ounces (US Treasury, as of {F['gozd']}), full backing works out to about <strong>{F['imp_g']}</strong> per ounce of gold. Using all COMEX vault silver ({F['s_tot']}, {rd}), it's about <strong>{F['imp_s']}</strong> per ounce of silver. These are illustrations, not price targets.</p></section>
{PAPER_W}
{M1_W}
<section><h2>What M1 and M2 are</h2><p><strong>M1</strong> is the narrowest common measure of money: currency in circulation plus checking accounts and other balances you can spend immediately. <strong>M2</strong> adds savings deposits, small time deposits and retail money market funds. Both are published monthly by the Federal Reserve and available on FRED (series M1SL and M2SL).</p></section>
<section><h2>How to read the calculators</h2><p>The "paper ratio" is the implied price divided by today's spot price. A paper ratio of 20 means there are about 20 times as many dollars in M2 as the dollar value of the gold reserve at today's price. The backing tiers (30%, 40%, 50%) show what partial backing would imply, since historical gold standards rarely backed every dollar.</p><p>There's an important difference between the two metals. The US government holds an official gold reserve, but it holds no official silver reserve. So for silver we compare the money supply with all silver in COMEX vaults (registered plus eligible). That's a reference pile of metal, not something the government owns.</p></section>
<section><h2>Limits of this comparison</h2><p>The US left the gold standard decades ago, and nothing suggests the dollar will be re-backed by metal. Money supply changes monthly, reserves are reported with a lag, and spot prices move constantly, so every figure here carries its own date. The calculation is a way to put the size of the money supply in perspective. It isn't a valuation model and shouldn't be read as a forecast.</p></section>
{links('/gold-silver-ratio','/comex-silver-inventory','/comex-gold-inventory')}
{SRC(('FRED: M2SL','https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/M2SL'),('FRED: M1SL','https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/M1SL'),('US Treasury: Status Report of U.S. Government Gold Reserve','https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/status-report-government-gold-reserve/'),('gold-api.com (spot)','https://gold-api.com'))}''',
 ['livepaper.js','m1calc.js'],
 faq=[('What is the implied gold price from M2?','US M2 money supply divided by US government gold reserve ounces. It is a hypothetical full-backing figure, not a price prediction.'),
      ('Why does the silver calculation use COMEX vault silver?','The US holds no official silver reserve, so total COMEX silver (registered plus eligible) is used as a reference stock.')],
 dataset={"name":"Money supply vs metal backing inputs","description":"M1, M2, US gold reserve ounces and COMEX silver totals used for hypothetical backing calculations.","distribution":{"@type":"DataDownload","encodingFormat":"application/json","contentUrl":B+"/data/latest.json"}},crumb='Paper to metal')
P['gold-silver-news']=page('gold-silver-news','Live Gold &amp; Silver News Stream | VaultPulse',
 'Live stream of gold and silver headlines from Google News, Investing.com and FXStreet, refreshed every 75 seconds, with alert icons for big moves.',
 'Live gold &amp; silver news', f'''<section><p>This page streams the latest gold and silver headlines from several public news feeds in one place. New stories slide in at the top with a NEW tag, and the box checks for updates about every 75 seconds. Hover over or tap the box to pause the scroll.</p></section>
{NEWS_W}
<section><h2>Where the headlines come from</h2><p>Our server collects headlines from Google News search results for gold and silver, plus the Investing.com and FXStreet commodity feeds. It removes duplicates, sorts the newest first and caches the list for a few minutes. Each headline links to the original article and shows its source and how long ago it was published. Some feeds publish their times inconsistently, so a story can look slightly newer than it is.</p></section>
<section><h2>What the alert icons mean</h2><p>The 🚨 icon appears when a headline contains words that usually describe a major event, such as "crash," "plunge," "record high" or "force majeure." The ⚠️ icon marks notable moves or scheduled market events, such as a price slip, Federal Reserve decisions or inflation (CPI) reports. These icons come from simple keyword matching. They aren't our judgment of how important a story is, and they're not signals to buy or sell.</p></section>
<section><h2>Reading market news carefully</h2><p>Headlines are written to be clicked, and many of them include forecasts or price targets from banks and analysts. We pass along the headline as published, but we don't endorse any forecast. For context on a big headline, compare it with the dated data elsewhere on VaultPulse: COMEX inventory, deliveries, premiums and the gold-to-silver ratio.</p><p>Prefer email? The VaultPulse daily brief rounds up the day's top headlines with a vault snapshot. <a href="/#newsletter">Subscribe on the homepage</a>. We also post a daily roundup on <a href="https://x.com/VaultPulseHQ">@VaultPulseHQ</a>.</p></section>
{links('/comex-silver-inventory','/gold-silver-ratio','/comex-force-majeure','/silver-premiums')}
{SRC(('Google News','https://news.google.com'),('Investing.com: Commodities news','https://www.investing.com/news/commodities-news'),('FXStreet','https://www.fxstreet.com'))}''',
 ['news.js'], crumb='Gold &amp; silver news')

